  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If \\(c\\) is a real number and the negative of one of the solutions of \\(x^2-3x+c=0\\) is a solution of \\(x^2+3x-c=0,\\) then the solutions of \\(x^2-3x+c=0\\) are",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(1,\\) \\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(-1,\\) \\(-2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(0,\\) \\(3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(0,\\) \\(-3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\frac32,\\) \\(\\frac32\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1976-p6.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "For such a root \\(r,\\) \\[ \\begin{aligned} r^2-3r+c&=0,\\\\ (-r)^2+3(-r)-c&=0. \\end{aligned} \\] Subtracting gives \\(2c=0.\\) Thus the first equation is \\(x(x-3)=0,\\) with roots \\(0\\) and \\(3.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Call the first root \\(r\\) and substitute \\(-r\\) into the second equation",
        "The two resulting equations differ only in the sign of \\(c\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
